{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Equiv","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.NonsingularInverse"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv.card_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv.card_eq","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/MatRepEquiv.lean#L91-L97","doc":"**Equivalent representations have equal dimension.**  The `x = 1` instance of `trace_eq`,\nread through `CharZero` to land in `ℕ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv.trace_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv.trace_eq","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/MatRepEquiv.lean#L77-L89","doc":"**The character is an equivalence invariant.**  `tr ρ'(x) = tr ρ(x)` for every `x`.\n\nThis is the workhorse for *un*conditional separation, and it is strictly stronger than\n`scalar_eq`: it applies to every algebra element, not only those landing on scalars, so it can\ncompare whole spectra rather than one number.  Note it needs only `S * T = 1` and `T * S = 1` —\nno genericity, no algebra structure beyond the intertwining. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv.conj_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv.conj_eq","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/MatRepEquiv.lean#L68-L75","doc":"**An equivalence conjugates one representation into the other.**  The intertwining identity\n`ρ' x * S = S * ρ x` becomes `ρ' x = S * ρ x * T` once `S * T = 1` is used on the right. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv.scalar_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv.scalar_eq","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/MatRepEquiv.lean#L44-L66","doc":"**A scalar value is an equivalence invariant.**  If `x` is sent to a scalar matrix by both\nrepresentations, the two scalars agree.\n\nThis is the whole force of centrality: a central element goes to a scalar in every irreducible\nblock, and equivalent blocks cannot disagree about which scalar.  `Nonempty κ` is needed and is\nnot a formality — over the empty index type every matrix is `1`, so any two scalars would be\nindistinguishable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv","kind":"def","line":28,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/MatRepEquiv.lean#L28-L39","doc":"**Two matrix representations of one algebra are equivalent** when an invertible change of\nbasis intertwines them.\n\nStated with an explicit inverse pair `S`, `T` rather than `IsUnit S`, because the index types\n`ι` and `κ` differ: `S : Matrix κ ι R` is not square, so `IsUnit` does not apply and the two\none-sided identities are separate data.  Both are needed — `S * T = 1` is what forces the\nscalar comparison below, and `T * S = 1` is what makes this a symmetric notion. ","signatureChunk":0}]}